(TIM-11 - `unix` really is seconds since the epoch: the hour, minute and
 second properties are exactly the ones you get by dividing it up, which
 is the one oracle available here that needs no reference clock.
 It also settles which timezone the components are in - they agree with
 the UTC decomposition of `unix`, not with local time (TIM-44, D5).
 Also covers: TIM-44.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox TIM-11.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   hour agrees with the utc decomposition of unix
   minute agrees with the utc decomposition of unix
   second agrees with the utc decomposition of unix
)
Get current time into now.
a number called 'seconds into the day' is now's unix modulo 86400.
a number called 'the utc hour' is 'seconds into the day' divide 3600.
a number called 'the utc minute' is 'seconds into the day' divide 60 modulo 60.
a number called 'the utc second' is 'seconds into the day' modulo 60.
If now's hour is 'the utc hour' then, print "hour agrees with the utc decomposition of unix".
Otherwise, print "hour {now's hour} disagrees with utc hour {'the utc hour'}".
If now's minute is 'the utc minute' then, print "minute agrees with the utc decomposition of unix".
Otherwise, print "minute {now's minute} disagrees with utc minute {'the utc minute'}".
If now's second is 'the utc second' then, print "second agrees with the utc decomposition of unix".
Otherwise, print "second {now's second} disagrees with utc second {'the utc second'}".
